/// Lesson sections for the info page — never shown on the practice screen.
const List<TechniqueSection> techDownUpStrokesLesson = [
  TechniqueSection(
    title: "Why",
    body:
        "Full strokes and taps alone cannot play an accent pattern at speed: the hand that just played loud has to play soft next, and the hand that played soft has to be up in time for the next accent. Down and up strokes solve exactly that. Together with full stroke and tap they are the four strokes every accent pattern is built from (Moeller, Stone, Gladstone tradition).",
  ),
  TechniqueSection(
    title: "How",
    body:
        "Down stroke: start high, play the accent, and stop the stick low, just above the pad, by gently closing the back fingers. No rebound. Up stroke: start low, play a tap, and use that little motion to lift the stick high, ready for the next accent. So in R> R R R the right hand goes down, tap, tap, up. Every accent is prepared one note earlier.",
  ),
  TechniqueSection(
    title: "Tips",
    body:
        "Say the stroke names while you play line 1: \"down, tap, tap, up\". The most common fault is lifting late, the accent then rushes or comes out weak. Second most common: the stroke after the accent bounces up loud, stop it low. Line 8 walks the accent from 1 to e to & to a, one bar each.",
  ),
  TechniqueSection(
    title: "Song examples",
    body:
        "Superstition (Stevie Wonder): accents inside a sixteenth-note hi-hat. 50 Ways to Leave Your Lover (Paul Simon): Steve Gadd moves accents and soft strokes in one flowing motion. Drum-corps snare lines: down and up strokes at full speed.",
  ),
];
